## Summary

Rocket.Chat's `sendFileMessage` DDP method passes the entire attacker-supplied `file` object into `Uploads.updateFileComplete`, which merges it directly into a MongoDB `$set` update via `Object.assign`. There is no allow-list of writable fields. An attacker can therefore rewrite any column on their own upload record, notably `store` and the store-specific path fields.

When combined with a cloud-backed storage driver (Google Cloud Storage or Amazon S3), both of which read an attacker-controlled `file.<Provider>.path` as the object key, and the fact that the data-export pipeline stores ZIPs at a fully deterministic path (`{uniqueID}/uploads/userData/{userId}`, no random component), a regular user can force the server to mint a signed URL pointing at **any target user's data export**.

## Pre-requisites

- Any authenticated, non-admin account on the target instance.
- The target instance uses a cloud storage backend for file uploads: **Google Cloud Storage** or **Amazon S3**. Both are vulnerable via the same primitive (the attacker-controlled path field just changes name: `GoogleStorage.path` vs `AmazonS3.path`, and the `store` value changes to `GoogleCloudStorage:UserDataFiles` or `AmazonS3:UserDataFiles` respectively). GridFS / FileSystem backends are not directly vulnerable to GCS/S3 path hijack but share the same root-cause `Object.assign` primitive; they would need a separate path-traversal-style exploit to be useful.
- No shared channel with the victim and no knowledge of the victim's channels is required. The attacker operates entirely in their own private staging room. Only the victim's `userId` is needed (trivially enumerable via `users.info?username=...` or shared-channel member lists).
- The victim must have triggered a data export (Account, Preferences, "Export My Data" / "Download My Data") at any point in the past. The resulting ZIP is stored in the cloud bucket at the predictable `UserDataFiles` path and is **never deleted or expired**. Each new export overwrites the same key, so even a single historical export from years ago is still present and exfiltrable today.

## Root Cause

**1. Unfiltered update in the `sendFileMessage` code path:**
[`apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/methods/sendFileMessage.ts#L48`](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/methods/sendFileMessage.ts#L48), inside `parseFileIntoMessageAttachments` ([L34-L53](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/methods/sendFileMessage.ts#L34-L53)), invoked by the DDP `sendFileMessage` method (signature `(roomId, _store, file, msgData)` at [L162](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/methods/sendFileMessage.ts#L162)):
```ts
await Uploads.updateFileComplete(file._id, user._id, omit(file, '_id'));
```
Only `_id` is stripped from the attacker-supplied `file` object; every other field is forwarded verbatim.

8.3.x added a sibling check at [L41-L46](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/methods/sendFileMessage.ts#L41-L46) (`Uploads.findOneByIdAndUserIdAndRoomId(file._id, user._id, roomId, ...)`) that asserts the upload exists and belongs to the calling user in the named room. This does **not** prevent the attack: the attacker controls all three identifiers (their own staging-room id, their own user id, and the file id of the upload they just created), so the check passes and the unfiltered `updateFileComplete` call on the next line still runs.

**2. Blind merge into `$set`:**
[`packages/models/src/models/BaseUploadModel.ts#L67`](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/packages/models/src/models/BaseUploadModel.ts#L67), inside `updateFileComplete` ([L49-L74](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/packages/models/src/models/BaseUploadModel.ts#L49-L74)):
```ts
update.$set = Object.assign(file, update.$set);
// ...
return this.updateOne(filter, update);
```
`Object.assign(file, update.$set)` mutates `file` in place and returns it, so the caller-supplied `file` object (minus `_id`) is promoted to be the entire `$set` payload for the Mongo `updateOne`. There is no field allow-list.

**3. Attacker-controlled cloud storage path - GCS, S3, and WebDAV all read the path from the file record:**

[`apps/meteor/app/file-upload/ufs/GoogleStorage/server.ts#L45-L56`](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/ufs/GoogleStorage/server.ts#L45-L56):
```ts
this.getPath = function (file) {
    if (file.GoogleStorage) {
        return file.GoogleStorage.path;            // fully attacker-controlled after step 2
    }
    // Compatibility
    if (file.googleCloudStorage) {
        return file.googleCloudStorage.path + file._id;
    }
    return file._id;
};
```
[`ufs/GoogleStorage/server.ts#L58-L67`](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/ufs/GoogleStorage/server.ts#L58-L67), `getRedirectURL` passes the result straight into a GCS signed URL:
```ts
this.getRedirectURL = async (file, forceDownload = false) => {
    // ...
    const res = await bucket.file(this.getPath(file)).getSignedUrl(params);
    return res[0];
};
```

[`apps/meteor/app/file-upload/ufs/AmazonS3/server.ts#L70-L81`](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/ufs/AmazonS3/server.ts#L70-L81), identical pattern on the S3 driver:
```ts
this.getPath = function (file) {
    if (file.AmazonS3) {
        return file.AmazonS3.path;                 // fully attacker-controlled
    }
    // Compatibility
    if (file.s3) {
        return file.s3.path + file._id;
    }
    return file._id;
};
```

[`apps/meteor/app/file-upload/ufs/Webdav/server.ts#L53-L59`](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/ufs/Webdav/server.ts#L53-L59), same shape:
```ts
this.getPath = function (file) {
    if (file.Webdav) {
        return file.Webdav.path;
    }
    return file._id;
};
```

All three drivers pass their attacker-controlled `getPath` result straight into the provider call that serves the file. Mass assignment writes `file.GoogleStorage`, `file.AmazonS3`, or `file.Webdav` (whichever matches the configured backend) alongside `file.store`, and the dispatcher picks the matching provider.

**4. Predictable `UserDataFiles` path:**
[`apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/lib/FileUpload.ts#L93-L100`](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/lib/FileUpload.ts#L93-L100):
```ts
UserDataFiles() {
    return {
        collection: UserDataFiles,
        getPath(file: IUpload) {
            return `${settings.get('uniqueID')}/uploads/userData/${file.userId}`;
        },
        // ...
    };
},
```
The path is entirely a function of `settings.get('uniqueID')` (a per-instance identifier, same for every file on the instance, leaked into every signed URL any authenticated user ever sees, and therefore trivially discoverable) and the target user's `_id` (enumerable via `/api/v1/users.info`). There is no random-per-export component.

**5. Authorisation bypass by store-swap:**
[`apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/lib/requests.ts#L22-L62`](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/lib/requests.ts#L22-L62) handles `/file-upload/<fileId>/...`:
```ts
const file = await Uploads.findOneById(match[1]);
if (file) {
    if (!(await FileUpload.requestCanAccessFiles(req, file))) {
        res.writeHead(403); res.end(); return;
    }
    // ...
    await FileUpload.get(file, req, res, next);
}
```
`requestCanAccessFiles` (at [`FileUpload.ts#L451-L537`](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/lib/FileUpload.ts#L451-L537)) checks membership/access of `file.rid`, the room the upload was created in, i.e. the **attacker's own staging room**, so the check passes. `FileUpload.get` (at [`FileUpload.ts#L579-L586`](https://github.com/RocketChat/Rocket.Chat/blob/8.3.2/apps/meteor/app/file-upload/server/lib/FileUpload.ts#L579-L586)) then calls `getStoreByName(file.store)` and dispatches to whichever store the attacker injected. The store's `get` / `getRedirectURL` reads `file.GoogleStorage.path` (or `file.AmazonS3.path`, etc.) from the same poisoned record and signs a URL against the victim's object.

## Impact

The UserDataFiles export contains the victim's entire message history including **private channels the attacker has no membership in**, plus DMs, uploaded files, and profile metadata.
